#!/bin/sh
# pre-push — `git push` is not the team's publish path. git-publish.py is.
#
# WHY THIS EXISTS
#
# Repo rulesets can require GitHub-verified signatures on every commit
# that merges (`required_signatures`), and GitHub evaluates that at MERGE
# time, not push time — so the failure arrives long after the push, with
# every check green, as an unmergeable PR. Meanwhile a pushed commit is
# unsigned forever: a GitHub App bot has no account settings, so no
# signing key can ever be registered against it, and the only commit
# GitHub verifies for an App is one it creates itself through the API
# (no author, committer or signature field). That makes `git push` not
# merely the wrong choice but an unrecoverable one from a bot identity —
# and every observed `git push` here ended as a full branch rewrite later
# (a forced replay that dismissed every standing approval, re-ran CI, and
# cost the human another approval of byte-identical content).
#
# Observed on a ruleset-gated repo, a PR that needed FOUR review rounds:
# the commit fix was pushed (unsigned; DCO "repair"), pushed again
# (signature repair), replayed via the API, then re-replayed after a
# rebase — each rewrite dismissing the human's approval. All of it came
# from one place: a push that should have been a publish.
#
# WHY IT REFUSES (unlike the DCO hook, which never fails)
#
# The DCO hook adds a trailer because CI is the enforcement and the
# commit is usable either way. A push has no redeeming fallback: its
# commits cannot be repaired in place, only rewritten. So this hook
# refuses outright and points at `git-publish.py`, which produces the
# same commits as GitHub-signed ones on the first try.
#
# SCOPE AND ESCAPE
#
# Refusal applies to GitHub remotes (every spelling: https, ssh,
# git@) from every profile's tool-home, because the reason holds
# wherever the target repo happens to sit today. Non-GitHub remotes
# pass. A deliberate push that git-publish.py cannot express is not
# refused by design when run with HERMES_ALLOW_PUSH=1 — but that escape
# is for the operator reading this message, not for automating around
# the refusal.
#
# Like every hook under /opt/hermes-git-hooks, this lives behind the
# per-home global core.hooksPath and is skipped by --no-verify. Unlike
# the DCO hook it is unconditional: there is no repo state that makes a
# push the right tool here.
#
# git calls this as: pre-push <remote-name> <remote-url>, with the
# ref/sha pairs it wants to update on stdin (drained below).

url="${2:-}"
# Drain stdin unconditionally: git is already writing the ref-update
# list, and a hook that exits before reading risks EPIPE on the writer.
cat >/dev/null 2>/dev/null

case "$url" in
  github.com:*|git@github.com:*|ssh://git@github.com:*|ssh://git@github.com/*|https://github.com/*|http://github.com/*) ;;
  *) exit 0 ;;
esac

[ "${HERMES_ALLOW_PUSH:-}" = "1" ] && exit 0

cat >&2 <<'EOF'

git push: REFUSED by the container's pre-push hook — publish instead.

Commits created by a push can never carry a GitHub signature: a GitHub
App has no account settings, so no signing key can be registered, and
the only commit GitHub verifies for an App is one it creates itself
through the API. A repo requiring signed commits (required_signatures
in a branch ruleset) blocks the merge with every check green, and the
only repair is a full branch rewrite — which dismisses every standing
approval and re-runs CI.

Publish with:  git-publish.py [-C <worktree>] [-b <branch>]
  Replays the branch's own commits as API-created ones that GitHub
  signs (preserving messages, diffs, modes, deletes; re-pointing each
  Signed-off-by at the identity actually stamped). First run creates
  the remote branch and `gh pr create` works straight after it.
  An existing branch of unsigned commits (already published): repair it
  with  git-publish.py --replay-from <fork-point> --force .

A deliberate `git push` is still possible exactly once, on purpose:
  HERMES_ALLOW_PUSH=1 git push ...

EOF
exit 1